,"p140","p141","p142","p143","p144","p145","p146","p147","p148","p149","p150","p151","p152","p153","p154","p155","p156","p157","p158","p159","p160","p161","p162","p163","p164","p165","p166","p167","p168","p169","p170","p171","p172","p173","p174","p175","p176","p177","p178","p179","p180","p181","p182","p183","p184","p185","p186","p187","p188","p189","p190","p191","p192","p193","p194","p195","p196","p197","p198","p199","p200","p201","p202","p203","p204","p205","p206","p207","p208","p209","p210","p211","p212","p213","p214","p215","p216","p217","p218","p219","p220","p221","p222","p223","p224","p225","p226","p227","p228","p229","p230","p231","p232","p233","p234","p235","p236","p237","p238","p239","p240","p241","p242","p243","p244","p245","p246","p247","p248","p249","p250","p251","p252","p253","p254","p255","p256","p257","p258","p259","p260","p261","p262","p263","p264","p265","p266","p267","p268","p269","p270","p271","p272","p273","p274","p275","p276","p277","p278","p279","p280","p281","p282","p283","p284","p285","p286","p287","p288","p289","p290","p291","p292","p293","p294","p295","p296","p297","p298","p299","p300","p301","p302","p303","p304","p305","p306","p307","p308","p309","p310","p311","p312","p313","p314","p315","p316","p317","p318","p319","p320","p321","p322","p323","p324","p325","p326","p327","p328","p329","p330","p331","p332","p333","p334","p335","p336","p337","p338","p339","p340","p341","p342","p343","p344","p345","p346","p347","p348","p349","p350","p351","p352","p353","p354","p355","p356","p357","p358","p359","p360","p361","p362","p363","p364","p365","p366","p367","p368","p369","p370","p371","p372","p373","p374","p375","p376","p377","p378","p379","p380","p381","p382","p383","p384","p385","p386","p387","p388","p389","p390","p391","p392","p393","p394","p395","p396","p397","p398","p399","p400","p401","p402","p403","p404","p405","p406","p407","p408","p409","p410","p411","p412","p413","p414","p415","p416","p417","p418","p419","p420","p421","p422","p423","p424","p425","p426","p427","p428","p429","p430","p431","p432","p433","p434","p435","p436","p437","p438","p439","p440","p441","p442","p443","p444","p445","p446","p447","p448","p449","p450","p451","p452","p453","p454","p455","p456","p457","p458","p459","p460","p461","p462","p463","p464","p465","p466","p467","p468","p469","p470","p471","p472","p473","p474","p475","p476","p477","p478","p479","p480","p481","p482","p483","p484","p485","p486","p487","p488","p489","p490","p491","p492","p493","p494","p495","p496","p497","p498","p499","p500","p500","home"]}],"solution":"class BrowserHistory:\n    def __init__(self, homepage: str):\n        self.pages = [homepage]\n        self.cur = 0     # index of the current page\n        self.end = 0     # index of the newest valid page\n\n    def visit(self, url: str) -> None:\n        self.cur += 1\n        if self.cur < len(self.pages):\n            self.pages[self.cur] = url\n        else:\n            self.pages.append(url)\n        self.end = self.cur\n\n    def back(self, steps: int) -> str:\n        self.cur = max(0, self.cur - steps)\n        return self.pages[self.cur]\n\n    def forward(self, steps: int) -> str:\n        self.cur = min(self.end, self.cur + steps)\n        return self.pages[self.cur]\n","approach":"**Key insight: history is a linear timeline, so an array with a cursor beats two stacks.** `back` and `forward` become `cur = max(0, cur - steps)` and `cur = min(end, cur + steps)`, both O(1). Visiting writes at `cur + 1` and marks that as the new end, which logically discards the forward history without touching memory; later visits simply overwrite those stale slots.\n\nThe two-stack version (back stack, forward stack) is a fine first answer, but `visit` must empty the forward stack and `back(steps)` moves one element at a time, so it is O(steps) per call. A doubly linked list has the same O(steps) cost. The array with an `end` pointer is strictly better and just as short.\n\n**Common mistakes:** truncating the list with slicing on every visit (correct but O(n)); forgetting to clamp at the homepage or at the newest page; off-by-one when `steps` exceeds the available history.\n\n**Interview follow-ups:** Memory bound: real browsers cap the history length; use a ring buffer or drop the oldest page when the array exceeds a limit (shift indices accordingly). Multiple tabs: one instance per tab. Persisting across restarts: serialise the array and the cursor. What about `back` in O(1) when steps is huge? Already O(1) here, which is the point of the array approach over stacks.","complexity":{"time":"O(1) per operation","space":"O(number of pages visited)"},"similar":["min-stack","implement-queue-using-stacks"],"number":174}